All,

We have taken some steps to alleviate our availability problems with the kn=
uckles and valves.

Knuckles

We have qualified a new 2" Knuckle vendor and have placed orders for 2000 a=
nd 5000 units.  The 2000 unit order is forecast to arrive here in early Jan=
uary.  The 5000 unit order is forecast to arrive here in mid-February provi=
ded production can be completed before Chinese New Year.  In doing this we =
are taking a risk ordering an additional 5000 units without having seen the=
 quality of an initial volume order.  2" knuckle demand for last 12 months =
was about 14000 units.

Our regular vendor has conquered its quality issues and identified the next=
 most limiting production step which turns out to be the shells.  He is add=
ing tooling at a different foundry immediately to increase this output.  Hi=
s current nominal capacity is about 2600 units per month all sizes combined=
 which is our last 12 months demand.

The regular vendor is to air freight at the pace of 600 units per week all =
sizes combined until further notice.  If the new vendor delivers as promise=
d we should be able to get out of airfreighting as a routine in the first q=
uarter.

In the first quarter if all goes as planned we should have about 7 months o=
f product on hand.  If we can achieve this we should be able to suspend rat=
ioning assuming sales at last 12 months pace.

Valves

We have qualified a new supplier that has demonstrated the ability to ship =
with much shorter lead times than the old supplier.  All orders are current=
ly going to the new vendor.  We have currently on hand plus on order 12 mon=
ths of product.  If all vendors perform we should have in the first quarter=
 all valves necessary to serve 2015 demand assuming sales of the last 12 mo=
nths.

Let me know if you have any questions.

J

--_000_4414AEBBCFB2B648B49E5766D309768E8191325A60MAIL_
